 "title": "Make your business more appealing to affluent customers",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator Mark Satterfield explains that to attract affluent customers without spending more on ads, businesses must be remembered for one specific strength rather than a long list of services. He suggests testing your positioning by asking five people who know your business to complete the phrase 'The thing they're especially good at is'.",
 "key_points": [
  "Attracting affluent customers does not require a bigger advertising budget or a lower price.",
  "Businesses need to be remembered for one specific strength rather than a list of multiple offerings.",
  "When people recommend a business, they cite one memorable strength like speed or handling complications.",
  "Most businesses list excellent service, competitive prices, experienced professionals, and comprehensive solutions, which provides too much information and little memorability.",
  "Action step: Ask five people who know your business to complete the sentence: 'The thing they're especially good at is...'",
  "Do not give any suggestions when asking those five people.",
  "Getting five completely different answers indicates an opportunity to make your positioning much stronger."
 ],
 "content": "There’s a surprisingly effective way to make your business more appealing to affluent customers and it doesn’t require a bigger advertising budget or a lower price. What it does require is being remembered for something specific. So, think about the last time someone recommended a business to you. Chances are they didn’t give you a complete rundown of everything the company offered. They probably said something like, they’re the people to call when you need something done quickly or they’re particularly good at handling complicated situations. One memorable strength. Now, compare that with the way most businesses describe themselves. They offer excellent service, competitive prices, experienced professionals and a comprehensive range of solutions. I mean, that’s a lot of information, but very little is memorable. So, here’s something worth trying. Ask five people who know your business to complete the sentence. The thing they’re especially good at is. Now, don’t give them any suggestions and if you get five completely different answers, you may have an opportunity to make your positioning much stronger because when affluent customers have a particular need, you want your business to be the first one that comes to mind. So, if this was helpful and you’d like more ideas about attracting affluent customers, comment more and I’ll send you a link with information about all my books, my programs, my podcast, and even how to work with me personally. This is Mark Satterfield. Thanks for watching. Please like and subscribe if you haven’t already and of course I always appreciate you passing these videos along to others you know who are marketing their products or services to the affluent. Talk to you soon. Bye for now.",
